    "title": "Abilene's Cypress Street project named finalist for statewide award - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Shelly Womack ,\n7 hours ago\nABILENE, Texas ( KTAB/KRBC ) – Abilene’s Cypress Street Improvement Project has been named a finalist for Best Public Improvement in the 2026 Texas Downtown President’s Awards.\nCompleted in early summer 2026, the project transformed Cypress Street between North First and North Fifth streets with roadway and sidewalk improvements designed to enhance downtown accessibility and appeal.\nRibbon cutting celebrates new Cypress Plaza, street improvements Upgrades include two-way traffic, 20-foot-wide sidewalks, decorative lighting, new landscaping, seating, bike racks and an outdoor sound system.\n“The Cypress Street Improvement Project has significantly enhanced the character and functionality of downtown Abilene,” said Kate Alvarez.\nThe project was developed as part of Abilene’s 2016 downtown master plan and funded through the city’s Tax Increment Reinvestment Zone program. The improvements aim to improve pedestrian safety and accessibility while supporting downtown businesses, cultural attractions and community events.\nBehind the magic of Abilene’s Storybook Gardens The statewide awards program received 155 entries this year. Winners will be announced Nov. 4, 2026, during the President’s Awards Gala in Amarillo, held as part of the Texas Downtown Annual Conference.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed.\nFor the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KTAB - BigCountryHomepage.com.",
    "summary": "Ribbon cutting celebrates new Cypress Plaza, street improvements Upgrades include two-way traffic, 20-foot-wide sidewalks, decorative lighting, new landscaping, seating, bike racks and an outdoor sound system. “The Cypress Street Improvement Project has significantly enhanced the character and functionality of downtown Abilene,” said Kate Alvarez. The project was developed as part of Abilene’s 2016 downtown master plan and funded through the city’s Tax Increment Reinvestment Zone program. Behind the magic of Abilene’s Storybook Gardens The statewide awards program received 155 entries this year.",
